{"text":"Recent work supports treating diagnostic scores as protocol-dependent evidence. [Pereira et al., RecSys 2025](https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748086) evaluate negative sampling under simulated exposure biases, distinguishing model separability, agreement with full-catalog evaluation, robustness, and alignment with ground-truth preferences. [Prakash et al., 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.17276) find that aggregate sequential-recommendation metrics conceal head/mid/tail differences; training-negative strategies trade overall accuracy against popularity balance. Implication: stratify probes jointly by training-derived item popularity and history length; vary diagnostic candidate samples separately while leaving the pinned 99-negative evaluator unchanged.

Temporal leakage is another substantial confound. [Le et al., 2025](https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3759329) report sampled nDCG@10 reductions of 21.7–73.4% under leakage-preventing evaluation; [Time to Split, 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2507.16289) shows splitting choices can change model rankings. These findings motivate training-only chronological replay, with parameters, histories, popularity counts, and catalog eligibility restricted to each replay cutoff. Replay using a checkpoint trained on later events cannot establish leakage-free predictive performance.

The retrieved literature does not establish a validated, general-purpose fault-injection diagnostic suite for GRU4Rec. Proposed calibration: inject controlled order shuffling, recent-history truncation, embedding corruption, and incorrect padding readout; require relevant probes to respond consistently across severity levels. Repeat user subsampling and use paired user-level bootstrap intervals, distinguishing user uncertainty from candidate-sampling variability. Calibrate probes exclusively on training replay; afterward examine associations between probe changes and per-category validation HR@10. Preserve macro validation HR@10 as the selection criterion; diagnostic correlations alone do not establish causality or justify changing the evaluator."}
